}
\description{
Turn a sample sheet and a model (factors with reference levels, covariates with units, batch, interactions, strata) into mzLib's design per stratum: the design matrix, its rank and aliased terms, the default contrasts with their estimability, and the design and contrast tables stats fit reads.

mzLib's \code{AnalysisDesign.ByStratum} splits the samples by stratum, and in each stratum builds one
additive model: an intercept, each factor treatment-coded against its reference level, batch,
each covariate as \code{(value - centre) / scale}, and any interactions. The matrix equals R's
\code{model.matrix()} for the same formula. It then lists \code{DefaultContrasts} - each level against its
reference, every pair when there is no reference, each covariate's slope - and judges whether
each can be estimated at all from these samples.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_diff_design}:

  - \code{records}, a data.frame with one row per (stratum, default contrast), strata in order:
    \code{stratum}, the stratum key; \code{contrast_id}, \code{c1}, \code{c2}, ... numbered \strong{once over the whole
    analysis} (mzLib #1476), so an id is the same comparison in every stratum;
    \code{label}, \code{factor=level vs factor=reference}, or a slope's column; \code{numerator} and
    \code{denominator}, \code{factor=level} (\code{NA} for a slope); \code{covariate}, \code{covariate_unit} (the unit a
    slope is per, e.g. \code{decade}) and \code{covariate_scale} (e.g. \code{years/10, centred at 50 years}),
    \code{NA} when not a slope; \code{estimability}, \code{"estimable"}, \code{"not_estimable:confounded"} or
    \code{"not_estimable:rank_deficient"}; and two list columns, \code{weights} (contrast weight per design
    column, unitless), one per entry of the stratum's \code{column_names}, and \code{fitted_weights}
    (contrast weight per fitted column, unitless), one per entry of its \code{fitted_column_names} -
    the contrasts file's row - and \code{NULL} when the contrast is not estimable.
  - \code{strata}, a list of \code{mzlibr_design_stratum}, named by stratum key (\code{"all"} for one stratum,
    else the stratum columns sorted by name as \code{name=value} joined by \code{;}), each with:
    \code{sample_count} samples; \code{sample_ids}, the matrix rows, ordinal by sample id; \code{column_names},
    mzLib's columns (\code{intercept}, \code{factor=level} per non-reference level, \code{batch=level},
    \code{name (per unit)} for a covariate, \code{a=level:b=level} for an interaction); \code{fitted_column_names},
    the columns a fit takes (the design file's coefficient columns); \code{rank} (columns);
    \code{aliased_terms}, terms that share a direction with another; \code{warnings}; \code{not_run}, a
    data.frame of the contrasts this stratum cannot run (mzLib's \code{NotRunContrast}): \code{label},
    \code{reason} (\code{"not_in_stratum"}) and \code{id}, the default contrast's id, which this stratum skips
    (\code{NA} when no default contrast stands for the entry, such as a factor with one level over the
    whole analysis); \code{covariate_centres}
    (the covariate's stored unit), the centre subtracted from each covariate; \code{matrix} and
    \code{fitted_matrix}, R matrices with the sample ids as row names, in coding (0/1 indicators;
    covariate columns in scaled units), equal to R's \code{model.matrix()}; and \code{design_file} and
    \code{contrasts_file}, the files written for this stratum, \code{NA} without \code{out} (and
    \code{contrasts_file} \code{NA} when nothing here is estimable).
  - \code{samples_file}, as an absolute path; \code{sample_count} samples read, across every stratum;
    \code{factors}, \code{covariates} and \code{interactions} as given (a covariate field \code{NA} was not given, so
    mzLib's default applied); \code{stratum_columns}; \code{batch_column} (\code{NA} when batch is not in the
    model); \code{unused_columns}; \code{stratum_count} strata; \code{estimability_counts}, default contrasts
    (contrasts) per verdict across strata; \code{written}, \code{directory} and \code{file_count} (files), or
    \code{NULL} without \code{out}; \code{row_count} rows; \code{column_names}.
  - \code{caveats}: how the matrix is coded and what the contrast ids mean, plus counts of contrasts
    that cannot be estimated or run.

}
\section{When the samples cannot answer}{


A comparison the samples cannot separate - a condition run in only one batch - is
\code{"not_estimable:confounded"}, and one that needs a column no sample informs (an empty interaction
cell) is \code{"not_estimable:rank_deficient"}. mzLib says so instead of returning a number. The design
file still holds a design that can be fitted: \code{fitted_column_names} leaves out the later of two
aliased columns. Contrast ids are numbered once over every sample of the analysis, so \code{c1} is
the same comparison in every stratum; a stratum without samples on both sides skips the id and
lists it in \code{not_run}.

}
