\section{Parameters: units, ranges and defaults}{
\describe{
\item{\code{samples}}{path; required. The sample sheet: a TSV with a header row and one row per biological sample. The first column is the sample id (unique, any header). Every other column is available to the model by its header; a column the model does not name is ignored and listed in unused_columns. Cells are trimmed. A factor, stratum or batch cell is used as text, exactly as written. A covariate cell is a number in the covariate's stored unit; a blank, NA or NaN covariate cell is no value, and mzLib refuses that sample by name.}
\item{wire \code{--stdin}}{string[]; required; range \code{one model term per line; fields tab-separated}. The model, one term per line, its fields separated by tabs; an empty optional field is not given, so mzLib's default applies. factor NAME, optionally then REFERENCE: a condition factor treatment-coded against REFERENCE (FactorSpec); with no REFERENCE every pair of its levels is compared and mzLib warns. covariate NAME UNIT, optionally then SCALE, SCALED_UNIT and CENTRE: a numeric column entered as (value - CENTRE) / SCALE (CovariateSpec). UNIT is the stored unit (years); SCALE is stored units per scaled unit (10), default 1; SCALED_UNIT names the unit a slope is per (decade), default SCALE stored units; CENTRE is in the stored unit, default the stratum's mean. interaction FIRST SECOND: the two-factor interaction of two named factors. stratum NAME: a column whose value splits the samples into strata, each designed on its own. batch NAME: the column holding each sample's batch, fitted as a fixed block whenever it is named (mzLib's DesignSpec.FitBatch default; the bridge never sets it). Factors and covariates enter in the order given. A column has one role, and is named once. \emph{Not an argument here: the model is the factors, covariates, interactions, strata and batch arguments, sent one stdin line per term.}}
\item{\code{out}}{path; default absent. A directory (created if absent) to write the pivot files into: for stratum i in the order of strata, design_i.tsv and, when any contrast is estimable there, contrasts_i.tsv. Files are numbered, not named by stratum key, because a key can hold any character a sheet cell can; each strata entry names its files. Files of the same name are overwritten. Absent, nothing is written.}
}
}
\section{Returned fields}{
Each field with its type, its unit, and what \code{NA} means when it is \code{NA}.

\describe{
\item{\code{samples_file}}{string; never \code{NA}. Absolute path of the sample sheet.}
\item{\code{sample_count}}{int; in \strong{samples}; never \code{NA}. Samples read from the sheet, across every stratum.}
\item{\code{factors}}{object[]; never \code{NA}. The factors given, in order: name and reference_level (null when none was given, so every pair of levels is compared).}
\item{\code{covariates}}{object[]; never \code{NA}. The covariates given, in order: name, unit, and scale, scaled_unit and centre as given (each null when not given, so mzLib's default applied).}
\item{\code{interactions}}{object[]; never \code{NA}. The interactions given, in order, each as first and second.}
\item{\code{stratum_columns}}{string[]; never \code{NA}. The stratum columns given; empty for one stratum.}
\item{\code{batch_column}}{string; \code{NA} when no batch line: batch is not in the model. The batch column given.}
\item{\code{unused_columns}}{string[]; never \code{NA}. Sheet columns after the sample id that the model does not name, in sheet order.}
\item{\code{stratum_count}}{int; in \strong{strata}; never \code{NA}. Designs built: one per distinct stratum key, or 1.}
\item{\code{strata}}{object[]; never \code{NA}. One object per stratum, in mzLib's order (ordinal by stratum key); fields under result.tables.strata.}
\item{\code{estimability_counts}}{map<string,int>; in \strong{contrasts}; never \code{NA}. Default contrasts per verdict across strata: estimable, not_estimable:confounded, not_estimable:rank_deficient.}
\item{\code{written}}{object; \code{NA} when no \code{--out}: nothing was written. directory (absolute) and file_count, the design and contrast files written.}
\item{\code{row_count}}{int; in \strong{rows}; never \code{NA}. Default contrasts across strata: the table's row count.}
\item{\code{column_names}}{string[]; never \code{NA}. The table's columns, in order.}
\item{\code{records} (wire \code{columns})}{table; never \code{NA}. One row per (stratum, default contrast): strata in order, each stratum's contrasts in mzLib's order.}
\item{\code{caveats}}{string[]; never \code{NA}. Five static caveats, plus one when any contrast is not estimable (with counts) and one when any stratum cannot run a contrast the model asks for.}
}
}
\section{Columns of \code{records}}{
\describe{
\item{\code{stratum}}{string; never \code{NA}. The stratum key (AnalysisDesign.StratumKey): the stratum columns sorted by name, each name=value, joined by ';', with \%, ; and = in a value escaped as \%25, \%3B and \%3D; all when there is no stratum column.}
\item{\code{contrast_id}}{string; never \code{NA}. c1, c2, ... in mzLib's default order, numbered once over every sample of the analysis (AnalysisDesign.ByStratum, since mzLib #1476), so an id is the same comparison in every stratum. The name a contrasts file gives the contrast.}
\item{\code{label}}{string; never \code{NA}. Readable: factor=level vs factor=reference for a level, the covariate's column name for a slope (age (per decade)).}
\item{\code{numerator}}{string; \code{NA} when a slope. The numerator level as factor=level.}
\item{\code{denominator}}{string; \code{NA} when a slope. The reference (or earlier) level as factor=level.}
\item{\code{covariate}}{string; \code{NA} when not a slope. For a slope, the covariate.}
\item{\code{covariate_unit}}{string; \code{NA} when not a slope. For a slope, the unit the effect is per: SCALED_UNIT, or SCALE stored units (10 years), or the stored unit at scale 1.}
\item{\code{covariate_scale}}{string; \code{NA} when not a slope. For a slope, mzLib's statement of how the stored covariate became that unit, e.g. years/10, centred at 50 years.}
\item{\code{estimability}}{string; never \code{NA}. mzLib's verdict: estimable | not_estimable:confounded (the direction it needs is shared by two or more terms, e.g. a condition only ever in one batch) | not_estimable:rank_deficient (it needs a column no sample informs, e.g. an empty interaction cell). Judged on the columns divided by their lengths, so the same in any unit.}
\item{\code{weights}}{float[]; in \strong{contrast weight per design column (unitless)}; never \code{NA}. One weight per entry of the stratum's column_names: the contrast is the weighted sum of the coefficients.}
\item{\code{fitted_weights}}{float[]; in \strong{contrast weight per fitted column (unitless)}; \code{NA} when not estimable: no fit gives it an estimate. AnalysisDesign.FittedWeights: the weights on the stratum's fitted_column_names, the row the contrasts file writes. A fit of the fitted matrix gives with them the estimate and standard error every least-squares fit of the full matrix gives.}
}
}
\section{Fields of \code{strata}}{
a list of \code{mzlibr_design_stratum}, named by stratum key.

\describe{
\item{\code{stratum}}{string; never \code{NA}. The stratum key, as the table's stratum column.}
\item{\code{sample_count}}{int; in \strong{samples}; never \code{NA}. Samples in the stratum.}
\item{\code{sample_ids}}{string[]; never \code{NA}. The rows of matrix and of the design file, ordinal by sample id.}
\item{\code{column_names}}{string[]; never \code{NA}. mzLib's columns: intercept; factor=level for each non-reference level; batch=level; name (per unit) for a covariate; a=level:b=level for an interaction.}
\item{\code{fitted_column_names}}{string[]; never \code{NA}. AnalysisDesign.FittedColumns by name: every column at full rank, else each column kept unless it is redundant with those kept before it. The design file's coefficient columns.}
\item{\code{rank}}{int; in \strong{columns}; never \code{NA}. The numerical rank, by LinearModel's own full-rank test: the number of fitted columns.}
\item{\code{aliased_terms}}{string[]; never \code{NA}. Terms (factor names, batch, covariate names, intercept) that share a direction with another term and so cannot be separated from it; empty at full rank.}
\item{\code{warnings}}{string[]; never \code{NA}. What mzLib left out of the model or changed from the specification here, in plain words: a factor with one level, a reference level with no sample, one batch, no reference declared.}
\item{\code{not_run}}{object[]; never \code{NA}. Contrasts the model asks for that this stratum cannot run (mzLib's NotRunContrast), each with id (the default contrast's id, the same in every stratum; null when no default contrast stands for the entry, such as a factor with one level over the whole analysis), label and reason (not_in_stratum).}
\item{\code{covariate_centres}}{map<string,float>; in \strong{the covariate's stored unit}; never \code{NA}. The centre subtracted from each covariate here: the one given, else the stratum's mean.}
\item{\code{matrix}}{float[][]; in \strong{coding (0/1 indicators; covariate columns in scaled units)}; never \code{NA}. AnalysisDesign.Matrix, one row per sample_ids entry and one value per column_names entry. Equals R's stats::model.matrix for the same formula and level order.}
\item{\code{fitted_matrix}}{float[][]; in \strong{coding (0/1 indicators; covariate columns in scaled units)}; never \code{NA}. AnalysisDesign.FittedMatrix: matrix with only the fitted columns. It has full column rank, so LinearModel.Fit takes it; it is the design file's body.}
\item{\code{design_file}}{string; \code{NA} when no \code{--out}. Absolute path of design_i.tsv: header sample then fitted_column_names, then one row per sample, the fitted matrix in round-trip decimal. Exactly what stats fit \code{--design} reads.}
\item{\code{contrasts_file}}{string; \code{NA} when no \code{--out}, or no contrast is estimable in this stratum. Absolute path of contrasts_i.tsv: header contrast then fitted_column_names (the design file's coefficient columns, same order), then one row per estimable contrast: contrast_id and fitted_weights. Each row is the ContrastWeights(name, weights) mzLib's EmpiricalBayes.ModerateContrasts takes for a fit of the design file: exactly what stats fit \code{--contrasts} reads, beside \code{--design} design_i.tsv (pyMzLib 0.6.0).}
}
}
\section{Errors}{
Each is an R condition carrying the class shown and \code{mzlib_error}; see \code{\link{mzlib_error}}.

\describe{
\item{\code{mzlib_usage_error} (usage)}{\code{--samples} missing, blank or not found; \code{--out} given without a directory; the sheet empty, header-only, with an empty or repeated column name, an empty or repeated sample id (repeats across strata included), or a row whose field count differs from its header; a covariate cell that is not a number (other than blank, NA, NaN)}
\item{\code{mzlib_usage_error} (usage)}{a stdin line that is not factor, covariate, interaction, stratum or batch, or has the wrong number of fields; an empty name or unit; a SCALE or CENTRE that is not a finite number; a column named twice or in two roles; a second batch line; an interaction given twice; a named column the sheet does not have}
\item{\code{mzlib_usage_error} (usage)}{mzLib refuses the input (its ArgumentException, reclassified): a sample with no value for a factor or covariate, a non-positive scale, batch on some samples only, an interaction naming a factor that is not in the model}
\item{\code{mzlib_bridge_error} (correctness)}{never expected}
\item{\code{mzlib_service_unavailable} (service_unavailable)}{Never raised by this verb.}
}
}
\section{Caveats}{
\itemize{
\item Each stratum's matrix is mzLib's AnalysisDesign: an intercept; each factor treatment-coded against its reference level, its other levels in ordinal order; batch against its first level; each covariate as (value - centre) / scale; then any interactions. It equals R's stats::model.matrix for the same formula and level order.
\item Only mzLib's default contrasts are listed (AnalysisDesign.DefaultContrasts): each level against its factor's reference, every pair of levels when a factor has no reference, and each covariate's slope. With an interaction in the model, a factor's contrast is its effect at the reference level of the other factor.
\item Contrast ids (c1, c2, ...) are numbered once over every sample of the analysis (AnalysisDesign.ByStratum), so an id names the same comparison in every stratum. A stratum without samples on both sides of a contrast skips its id and lists it in not_run.
\item A design file holds only the fitted columns (fitted_column_names): mzLib leaves out a column that is redundant with earlier ones, because LinearModel.Fit refuses a design without full column rank. A contrast that is not estimable has no fitted weights and is not in the contrasts file.
\item Strata are never columns: each stratum gets its own design, centres and fit; only the default contrasts' ids are shared. The individual a sample came from is never a fixed column either; it is for a mixed model's random intercept.
}
}
